transcript.text:
Here's the complete workflow — two API calls: one to create the transform, one to preview its output against a real identity.

---

## Step 1 — Get an access token

```bash
curl -s -X POST "https://{tenant}.api.identitynow.com/oauth/token" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  --data-urlencode "grant_type=client_credentials" \
  --data-urlencode "client_id=YOUR_CLIENT_ID" \
  --data-urlencode "client_secret=YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET" \
  | jq -r '.access_token'
```

Store the result as `$TOKEN`.

---

## Step 2 — Create the transform

I'll show two examples. Pick one (or both).

### Option A — `upper` (uppercase a source attribute)

```bash
curl -s -X POST "https://{tenant}.api.identitynow.com/v3/transforms" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "name": "upper-email-prefix",
    "type": "upper",
    "attributes": {
      "input": {
        "type": "accountAttribute",
        "attributes": {
          "sourceName": "Active Directory",
          "attributeName": "mail"
        }
      }
    }
  }'
```

### Option B — `concat` (join first name + last name)

```bash
curl -s -X POST "https://{tenant}.api.identitynow.com/v3/transforms" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "name": "concat-display-name",
    "type": "concat",
    "attributes": {
      "values": [
        {
          "type": "accountAttribute",
          "attributes": {
            "sourceName": "Active Directory",
            "attributeName": "givenName"
          }
        },
        " ",
        {
          "type": "accountAttribute",
          "attributes": {
            "sourceName": "Active Directory",
            "attributeName": "sn"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }'
```

The response includes the transform `id` you'll reference when wiring it into an identity profile mapping.

---

## Step 3 — Preview the transform output against a real identity

The beta `identity-preview` endpoint runs the transform in-memory against a real identity without persisting anything — exactly what you want before committing it to an identity profile.

```bash
curl -s -X POST "https://{tenant}.api.identitynow.com/beta/identity-profiles/identity-preview" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "X-SailPoint-Experimental: true" \
  -d '{
    "identityId": "IDENTITY_ID_TO_TEST_AGAINST",
    "identityAttributeConfig": {
      "enabled": true,
      "attributeTransforms": [
        {
          "identityAttributeName": "displayName",
          "transformDefinition": {
            "type": "concat",
            "attributes": {
              "values": [
                {
                  "type": "accountAttribute",
                  "attributes": {
                    "sourceName": "Active Directory",
                    "attributeName": "givenName"
                  }
                },
                " ",
                {
                  "type": "accountAttribute",
                  "attributes": {
                    "sourceName": "Active Directory",
                    "attributeName": "sn"
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }' | jq '.previewAttributes[] | select(.name == "displayName")'
```

The response contains `previewAttributes` — an array of `{ name, value, previousValue, ... }` objects. Filter to your target attribute to confirm the computed value matches expectations before wiring it up.

---

## Key things to know

| Point | Detail |
|---|---|
| `identityId` | Any active identity GUID — grab one from `GET /v3/identities?limit=1` |
| `transformDefinition` in the preview | You can inline the full transform definition here — no need to reference the saved one by name |
| `X-SailPoint-Experimental: true` | Required for the beta preview endpoint; omitting it returns 400 |
| Scope for create | `idn:transform:manage` |
| Scope for preview | `idn:identity-profile:manage` |

Once the preview value looks correct, go to **Identity Profiles → Mappings**, pick the target identity attribute, and select your named transform.

---

```answer-summary
endpoints:
  - method: POST
    path: /oauth/token
    api_version: oauth
  - method: POST
    path: /v3/transforms
    api_version: v3
  - method: POST
    path: /beta/identity-profiles/identity-preview
    api_version: beta
auth_flow: OAuth2 bearer token
required_scopes: [idn:transform:manage, idn:identity-profile:manage]
key_parameters: [name, type, attributes, identityId, identityAttributeConfig, attributeTransforms, transformDefinition, identityAttributeName]
```

